    <string name="prediction_info_title">Understand the estimate</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_error">Unable to load information about the estimate.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_intro_title">What this estimate shows</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_intro_body">This estimate gives a statistical indication of the canyon\'s likely flow for today, tomorrow, and the day after tomorrow. It is calculated directly in the app from an embedded model, but it does not replace a recent observation in the field.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_reading_title">How to read it</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_reading_p1">Each row corresponds to one day: today, tomorrow, and the day after tomorrow. The gauge indicates the likely flow between Dry and Flood.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_reading_p2">The closer the cursor gets to High, Very high, or Flood, the more the model estimates that the flow may become heavy.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_reading_p3">The confidence badge drops if the model hesitates between several levels or if the score is close to a boundary between two categories.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_reading_p4">This estimate should be read as preparation help, not as a safety guarantee or an instantaneous measurement of the real flow.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_data_title">What data is used</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_data_p1">The model combines recent daily weather, short-term forecasts, certain canyon characteristics, the watershed area when known, and past observations from the canyon or nearby areas.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_data_p2">Specifically, it accounts for recent rainfall, its duration, the season, temperature, recent snow, and observed behavior in the canyon, massif, or region.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_training_title">Training data</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_stat_total_observations">%1$s past observations used in total</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_stat_train_rows">%1$s observations for training</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_stat_calibration_rows">%1$s observations for final calibration</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_stat_test_rows">%1$s observations for the test set</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_stat_feature_count">%1$s variables considered by the model</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_stat_canyon_count">%1$s canyons represented in the embedded data</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_stat_massif_count">%1$s massifs represented in the embedded data</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_stat_region_count">%1$s regions represented in the embedded data</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_influence_title">What most influences the estimate</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_influence_p1">The factors below are among the families of variables that currently weigh most in the embedded model.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_limits_title">Important limitations</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_limits_p1">The model does not see everything: it does not directly observe the canyon\'s real condition, water intakes, releases, very local storms, or recent changes in the field.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_limits_p2">The estimate may be less relevant if the weather is incomplete, if the watershed is unknown, or if the canyon has few past observations and relies on a more general context.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_limits_p3">A recent on-site observation, feedback from practitioners, or field assessment remains more reliable than the estimate alone.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_good_use_title">Good use</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_good_use_p1">Use this feature to prepare an outing, compare several days, or understand whether a canyon seems rather calm, moderate, or heavy.</string>
    <string name="prediction_info_good_use_p2">Before a descent, always cross-check this estimate with the weather, the latest observed flow reports, the group\'s experience, and the real on-site conditions.</string>
